00:21:52.984 --> 00:21:56.944
micrometeoroids and orbital debris, specifically the

355
00:21:57.044 --> 00:21:59.304
design considerations related to these.

356
00:22:01.924 --> 00:22:05.544
So looking at micrometeoroids and orbital debris, or often

357
00:22:05.564 --> 00:22:08.684
abbreviated to MMOD, here's an overview.

358
00:22:09.964 --> 00:22:13.824
Orbital debris are going to be any man-made objects

359
00:22:13.864 --> 00:22:17.604
orbiting the Earth that no longer serve a useful purpose.

360
00:22:17.684 --> 00:22:21.444
So this might be the upper stages of rockets, defunct

361
00:22:21.544 --> 00:22:25.464
satellites, or fragments from satellite collisions or breakups that

362
00:22:25.504 --> 00:22:27.104
are still in Earth orbit.

363
00:22:28.244 --> 00:22:30.744
These objects can reach speeds up to

364
00:22:30.764 --> 00:22:34.744
17,500 miles per hour in low Earth orbit,

365
00:22:34.884 --> 00:22:38.604
which is roughly 10 times the average speed of a bullet.

366
00:22:38.704 --> 00:22:41.104
So extremely high velocities we're talking about.

367
00:22:42.644 --> 00:22:46.384
Going on to micrometeoroids. These are 10 micron to two

368
00:22:46.504 --> 00:22:49.744
millimeter natural solid objects that are moving through

369
00:22:49.784 --> 00:22:53.664
interplanetary space. But for the context of this presentation,

370
00:22:53.864 --> 00:22:57.804
we're primarily concerned with objects that have fallen into the

371
00:22:57.844 --> 00:23:01.144
gravitational well of the Earth and are in Earth

372
00:23:01.164 --> 00:23:04.954
orbit. In both cases, we're often talking about

373
00:23:05.024 --> 00:23:08.304
extremely small objects, but these can still

374
00:23:08.364 --> 00:23:12.224
be highly destructive when colliding with satellites at

375
00:23:12.284 --> 00:23:13.264
these very high

376
00:23:14.524 --> 00:23:17.924
relative velocities, which they very often

377
00:23:18.004 --> 00:23:18.624
are.

378
00:23:20.184 --> 00:23:23.723
Just looking at the evolution of orbital debris.

379
00:23:23.843 --> 00:23:27.244
As of 2024, the European Space Agency

380
00:23:27.324 --> 00:23:31.264
reported 36,240 debris

381
00:23:31.364 --> 00:23:33.444
objects in orbit, and

382
00:23:34.424 --> 00:23:37.944
that only accounts for the objects that they've been able

383
00:23:38.044 --> 00:23:42.004
to track. So typically, anything under about 10

384
00:23:42.024 --> 00:23:44.504
centimeters is not going to be trackable.

385
00:23:44.624 --> 00:23:48.514
So all of those smaller objects aren't counted in

386
00:23:48.564 --> 00:23:49.324
that number.

387
00:23:50.564 --> 00:23:54.293
This graph here, courtesy of the Clean Orbit Foundation,

388
00:23:54.884 --> 00:23:56.364
is looking at from

389
00:23:57.484 --> 00:24:01.304
the dawn of the space age in the early 1960s to

390
00:24:01.944 --> 00:24:03.004
roughly 2020.

391
00:24:04.064 --> 00:24:06.984
The near exponential increase

392
00:24:07.104 --> 00:24:10.584
in orbital debris objects that are in Earth

393
00:24:10.624 --> 00:24:14.344
orbit. Again,

394
00:24:14.444 --> 00:24:17.884
looking at all different types of fragments here, but we're

395
00:24:17.964 --> 00:24:21.834
not counting micrometeoroids in the count on

396
00:24:21.884 --> 00:24:22.424
this graph.

397
00:24:26.104 --> 00:24:29.944
Now considering the risks of MMOD objects.

398
00:24:31.124 --> 00:24:35.044
Looking at the size. So any objects that are going to be

399
00:24:35.184 --> 00:24:38.824
greater than 10 centimeters in size are

400
00:24:38.884 --> 00:24:42.414
trackable, but are too large for effective

401
00:24:42.624 --> 00:24:45.644
shielding. So the potential impact to a satellite

402
00:24:45.744 --> 00:24:49.704
is anywhere from catastrophic damage to complete

403
00:24:49.724 --> 00:24:50.404
destruction.

404
00:24:51.684 --> 00:24:55.664
Then going on to objects that are one to 10 centimeters in

405
00:24:55.764 --> 00:24:59.664
size. The larger objects in this range are

406
00:24:59.704 --> 00:25:03.504
trackable. However, they are still going to

407
00:25:03.544 --> 00:25:06.424
be too large for effective shielding against.

408
00:25:06.484 --> 00:25:06.544
So-

409
00:25:07.488 --> 00:25:11.148
We'll go on to talk about shielding in the design

410
00:25:11.368 --> 00:25:15.188
section of this presentation, but essentially that's an option for

411
00:25:15.248 --> 00:25:16.648
mitigating the risk

412
00:25:17.488 --> 00:25:20.028
due to an impact of an MMOD object.

413
00:25:21.488 --> 00:25:24.548
So looking at the potential impact to a satellite

414
00:25:25.008 --> 00:25:28.848
for the one to 10 centimeter object, that's going to

415
00:25:28.968 --> 00:25:32.448
be moderate to catastrophic damage to the

416
00:25:32.488 --> 00:25:33.028
satellite.

417
00:25:34.108 --> 00:25:38.068
Finally, looking at objects that are less than one centimeter

418
00:25:38.108 --> 00:25:41.268
in size. These objects are not trackable,

419
00:25:41.728 --> 00:25:45.008
however, effective shielding does exist.

420
00:25:45.188 --> 00:25:48.788
So the potential impact there is likely

421
00:25:48.798 --> 00:25:51.028
minor damage. However,

422
00:25:51.088 --> 00:25:54.928
these small amounts of damage can still be

423
00:25:54.948 --> 00:25:57.167
detrimental to a mission over time.

424
00:25:58.488 --> 00:26:02.348
Ultimately, the results of MMOD impacts

425
00:26:02.408 --> 00:26:06.388
can range from complete satellite destruction to minor erosions,

426
00:26:06.468 --> 00:26:10.268
cracks, and dents. However, even these minor effects

427
00:26:10.328 --> 00:26:14.288
can cause very long-term damage to a mission as they build up over

428
00:26:14.328 --> 00:26:18.028
time. The result of these impacts often

429
00:26:18.068 --> 00:26:21.588
depends on the MMOD object size

430
00:26:21.828 --> 00:26:25.388
and the relative velocity between an object and a

431
00:26:25.428 --> 00:26:26.648
satellite. But obviously,

432
00:26:27.508 --> 00:26:30.348
the larger the debris or

433
00:26:30.388 --> 00:26:34.208
micrometeoroid and the higher relative velocity

434
00:26:34.248 --> 00:26:38.228
between the object and the satellite, the worst damage is going to

435
00:26:38.288 --> 00:26:38.668
be done.

436
00:26:41.548 --> 00:26:45.528
Here is just an example of an MMOD impact on the ISS

437
00:26:45.608 --> 00:26:47.768
on one of the service module windows.

438
00:26:48.788 --> 00:26:52.228
A very small fragment hit one of the windows and was able to

439
00:26:52.268 --> 00:26:55.408
produce this chip in the window, which

440
00:26:55.768 --> 00:26:59.458
obviously over time these could build up and cause larger

441
00:26:59.508 --> 00:27:03.448
cracks or structural failures that could lead to extremely

442
00:27:03.468 --> 00:27:05.138
detrimental mission failures.

443
00:27:08.188 --> 00:27:11.648
Now going on to design considerations.

444
00:27:11.728 --> 00:27:15.678
So some of these considerations are used to

445
00:27:15.828 --> 00:27:16.398
mitigate

446
00:27:17.228 --> 00:27:20.968
the effects of an MMOD impact, while some

447
00:27:21.328 --> 00:27:22.528
are used to

448
00:27:23.348 --> 00:27:27.188
reduce the potential for an impact at

449
00:27:27.248 --> 00:27:27.408
all.

450
00:27:28.668 --> 00:27:29.168
So first,

451
00:27:30.728 --> 00:27:34.228
one consideration is designing satellites to just

452
00:27:34.308 --> 00:27:36.788
withstand impacts by small debris objects.

453
00:27:36.868 --> 00:27:40.258
So this can be done by careful material selection,

454
00:27:40.408 --> 00:27:44.148
obviously selecting materials that are going to be the most resilient

455
00:27:44.208 --> 00:27:47.068
to these impacts. And then

456
00:27:47.148 --> 00:27:50.988
also shielding on satellites. So these

457
00:27:51.088 --> 00:27:53.968
are typically sacrificial

458
00:27:54.728 --> 00:27:58.668
layers or just a single layer that are put in front

459
00:27:58.728 --> 00:28:02.528
of the structure of a spacecraft that absorb the shock

460
00:28:02.908 --> 00:28:06.888
from an impact. So a very common one

461
00:28:06.928 --> 00:28:08.668
of these is called a Whipple shield.

462
00:28:09.508 --> 00:28:13.358
The idea had been come up with in the 1940s and is still

463
00:28:13.408 --> 00:28:15.328
in use today. But essentially,

464
00:28:16.368 --> 00:28:19.988
typically aluminum is used to provide either one layer or

465
00:28:20.048 --> 00:28:23.988
multiple layers of shock-absorbing shields, basically

466
00:28:24.048 --> 00:28:26.168
in front of the main spacecraft structure.

467
00:28:27.828 --> 00:28:31.088
Additionally, another design consideration is to

468
00:28:31.768 --> 00:28:35.628
obviously maintain mission performance while reducing

469
00:28:35.788 --> 00:28:38.608
fragmentation from solar radiation.

470
00:28:38.668 --> 00:28:42.488
So we've talked about the space environment

471
00:28:42.528 --> 00:28:45.738
and the potential types of particles that can result in

472
00:28:45.768 --> 00:28:48.488
degradation of satellite materials.

473
00:28:48.688 --> 00:28:52.088
And as these materials degrade,

474
00:28:52.548 --> 00:28:56.468
they can result in fragmentation that

475
00:28:56.528 --> 00:29:00.108
essentially creates orbital debris and become a hazard for other

476
00:29:00.148 --> 00:29:04.078
satellites. So avoiding that is definitely a big

477
00:29:04.128 --> 00:29:06.048
consideration for mission design.

478
00:29:07.828 --> 00:29:11.568
Next, we can increase the quality and

479
00:29:11.608 --> 00:29:15.068
reliability of propellants and batteries to prevent

480
00:29:15.168 --> 00:29:19.048
accidental explosions. So this is another consideration that's

481
00:29:19.128 --> 00:29:23.068
aimed at reducing the

482
00:29:23.128 --> 00:29:27.048
number of debris-causing events in orbit and

483
00:29:27.108 --> 00:29:28.128
ultimately reducing

484
00:29:29.248 --> 00:29:31.708
the amount of debris created in general.

485
00:29:33.428 --> 00:29:33.748
Then

486
00:29:34.768 --> 00:29:38.468
we can look at improving maneuverabilities for

487
00:29:38.628 --> 00:29:42.288
collision avoidance. So in those cases of larger

488
00:29:42.348 --> 00:29:46.288
debris objects that you're not going to be able to shield against

489
00:29:46.408 --> 00:29:50.328
and that an impact from would result in complete destruction of a

490
00:29:50.388 --> 00:29:50.888
satellite,

491
00:29:51.908 --> 00:29:55.408
the best case scenario is maneuvering to avoid a collision.

492
00:29:55.508 --> 00:29:58.728
So improving a satellite's ability to do so is going to

493
00:29:58.768 --> 00:30:00.968
help avoid that risk.

494
00:30:01.788 --> 00:30:05.048
Then finally, post-mission disposal is a big one.

495
00:30:05.088 --> 00:30:08.808
So either graveyard orbits, which are less utilized

496
00:30:09.128 --> 00:30:12.728
orbits that a satellite could be inserted into or

497
00:30:12.788 --> 00:30:16.048
completely de-orbiting where a satellite is going to

498
00:30:16.168 --> 00:30:16.848
reenter

499
00:30:17.728 --> 00:30:21.228
the Earth's atmosphere. And again, these are

500
00:30:22.608 --> 00:30:25.408
design considerations that are aimed at

501
00:30:25.828 --> 00:30:26.648
reducing

502
00:30:27.448 --> 00:30:28.588
the

503
00:30:30.588 --> 00:30:34.488
number of debris objects in orbit and improving the quality of

504
00:30:34.528 --> 00:30:36.208
orbit for other missions.

505
00:30:37.808 --> 00:30:41.658
That essentially sums up the debris considerations and the rest of this

506
00:30:41.688 --> 00:30:45.088
presentation. My sources are all here,

507
00:30:45.128 --> 00:30:49.108
but hopefully we all learned something about micrometeoroids

508
00:30:49.148 --> 00:30:52.908
